# Define an Agent

## Overview

An Agent definition fixes the shape of an Agent's state before any Signal
arrives. Jido checks it when the module compiles, and checks every instance
against it, so a running Agent never holds state its schema doesn't allow.

By the end of this Livebook you'll have a profile Agent that does this:

```text
Define.Profile.instantiate!(id: "profile-1").state
#=> %{name: "Anonymous", tags: [], visits: 0}
```

You'll declare it with the DSL, create instances, update and validate them,
then build the same kind of Agent from a plain map.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Supporting Modules

The profile needs one Action and one Plugin. The Plugin owns a `visits` field
and adds 1 on every successful Turn. It's here to show how Plugin fields join
the state schema. [Stateful Plugins](write-a-stateful-plugin.livemd) covers
writing one.

```elixir
defmodule Define.Visits do
  use Jido.Plugin

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts), do: {:visits, Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}

  @impl true
  def reduce(reduction, _opts), do: {:ok, reduction.plugin_state + 1}
end

defmodule Define.Rename do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "rename",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(%{name: name}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | name: String.trim(name)}}
  end
end
```

## Declare The Agent

```elixir
defmodule Define.Profile do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "profile",
    description: "Owns one display profile",
    vsn: 2,
    metadata: %{domain: "profiles"},
    extensions: [Define.Visits]

  state do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        name: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("Anonymous"),
        tags: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])
      })
    )

    initial_state %{name: "Guest"}
  end

  @impl Jido.Agent
  def initialize(state, _context), do: {:ok, Map.update(state, :tags, [], &Enum.uniq/1)}

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/define")
    route "profile.rename", Define.Rename, as: :rename do
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

| Option | Where | Rule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | `use Jido.Agent` | Stable authoring Registry ID. Defaults to the module name. |
| `name` | `use Jido.Agent` | Required non-empty string. |
| `description` | `use Jido.Agent` | Optional string. |
| `vsn` | `use Jido.Agent` | Positive integer. Default `1`. |
| `schema` | `state` | A static Zoi object for domain state. |
| `initial_state` | `state` | An optional plain, static Map. |
| `extensions` | `use Jido.Agent` | Plugin and Extension modules with no options. |
| `metadata` | `use Jido.Agent` | A plain map of static data. |
| `plugin` | `plugins` | An ordered Plugin declaration with optional `config:`. |

Use an explicit `id` when the Agent can appear in a stored document. It must
use the shared authoring identifier format and remain stable when Elixir module
names change.

A bad route, schema, or Plugin fails the build.

You can also write `plugin(Define.Visits)` inside `plugins do` and pass options
with `config:`. This works in source files, generated modules, and Livebook
cells. Use `extensions:` when a provider adds custom Spark sections, or when it
needs no config.

## The Definition

`definition/0` returns the reusable blueprint. A definition has `id: nil`
and `state: nil`.

```elixir
definition = Define.Profile.definition()

{definition.name, definition.vsn, definition.metadata, definition.id,
 Jido.Agent.definition?(definition)}
```

The result is `{"profile", 2, %{domain: "profiles"}, nil, true}`.

A Map definition can contain reusable initial state:

```elixir
map_definition =
  Jido.Agent.define!(%{
    name: "map_profile",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("Anonymous")}),
    initial_state: %{name: "Guest"}
  })

map_definition.initial_state
```

The result is `%{name: "Guest"}`. The value must be a plain, static Map.

## Create An Instance

`instantiate!/1` adds an `id` and builds state from the schema defaults.

```elixir
agent = Define.Profile.instantiate!(id: "profile-1")

{agent.state, Jido.Agent.instance?(agent)}
```

The result is `{%{name: "Guest", tags: [], visits: 0}, true}`. The
`visits` field comes from the Plugin, not from your schema.

For a new instance, Jido merges `initial_state` with explicit `state:` values.
It then calls optional `initialize/2` with that candidate and a context that
contains `id`, `parent_id`, `member`, and a 1-based `slot`. The callback returns
the complete candidate as `{:ok, state}` or stops creation with
`{:error, reason}`. Jido then adds schema defaults and validates the state.

Pass `state:` to set initial values:

```elixir
agent2 = Define.Profile.instantiate!(id: "profile-2", state: %{name: "Ada", tags: ["admin"]})

agent2.state
```

The result is `%{name: "Ada", tags: ["admin"], visits: 0}`.

`instantiate/1` returns `{:ok, agent}` or `{:error, reason}`. Validation errors
use `%Jido.Agent.Error{}`. It rejects wrong types, unknown keys, and a missing `id`:

```elixir
{:error, wrong_type} = Define.Profile.instantiate(id: "p3", state: %{name: 42})
{:error, unknown_key} = Define.Profile.instantiate(id: "p3", state: %{nickname: "x"})
{:error, no_id} = Define.Profile.instantiate(state: %{})

{wrong_type.code, unknown_key.code, {no_id.path, Exception.message(no_id)}}
```

The result is `{:invalid_state, :invalid_state, {[:id], "Expected a nonempty string"}}`.
Each `Jido.Agent.Error` carries a `code`, a `path` to the bad field, and
`details`.

## Plugin-Owned Fields

Jido composes each Plugin's field into the state schema:

```elixir
Define.Profile.schema().fields |> Keyword.keys()
```

The result is `[:name, :tags, :visits]`.

Your Actions can read `visits`. Only the Plugin can change it. Run one rename:

```elixir
signal = Define.Profile.rename_signal!(%{name: " Grace "})
{:ok, renamed, []} = Define.Profile.cmd(agent, signal)

renamed.state
```

The result is `%{name: "Grace", tags: [], visits: 1}`. The Action trimmed the
name. The Plugin counted the Turn.

## Update With set/2

`Jido.Agent.set/2` replaces top-level domain fields outside a Turn and
validates the complete result. Use it in setup code and tests.

```elixir
{:ok, updated} = Jido.Agent.set(agent, name: "Linus")
{:error, plugin_field} = Jido.Agent.set(agent, visits: 9)

{updated.state.name, Exception.message(plugin_field)}
```

The result is `{"Linus", "Agent.set/2 accepts only domain state keys"}`.

A nested map you pass replaces the old map. To keep nested values, merge
first: `Jido.Agent.set(agent, config: Map.merge(agent.state.config, %{enabled: true}))`.

## Validate A Value

`definition?/1` and `instance?/1` check shape only. `Jido.Agent.validate/1`
checks fields, routes, Plugins, and complete state.

```elixir
broken = %{agent | state: %{name: "x"}}
{:error, error} = Jido.Agent.validate(broken)

{Jido.Agent.validate(agent) |> elem(0), Jido.Agent.instance?(broken), error.code}
```

The result is `{:ok, true, :incomplete_state}`. `broken` has instance shape
but is not a valid instance. Call `validate/1` on any `%Jido.Agent{}` you
build by hand.

## Define From A Map

`Jido.Agent.define/1` builds a definition from a plain map at runtime. It runs
the same validation as the DSL. It accepts a map, not a keyword list.

```elixir
{:ok, data_definition} =
  Jido.Agent.define(%{
    name: "profile_data",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("Anonymous")}),
    signal_source: "/guides/define",
    routes: [%{path: "profile.rename", handler: %{kind: :action, target: Define.Rename}}]
  })

{:ok, data_agent} = Jido.Agent.instantiate(data_definition, id: "data-1")
signal = Jido.Signal.new!("profile.rename", %{name: "Hopper"}, source: "/guides/define")
{:ok, data_candidate, []} = Jido.Agent.cmd(data_agent, signal)

{data_definition.module, data_candidate.state}
```

The result is `{nil, %{name: "Hopper"}}`. A map definition has no module, so
it has no generated helpers such as `rename_signal/2`. Build Signals with
`Jido.Signal.new!/3`. To store definitions as JSON, see
[Documents and Views](agent-documents-and-views.md).

## Schema Rules

State schemas validate values. They never convert them. Metadata must be
static data.

```elixir
{:error, coerced} =
  Jido.Agent.define(%{name: "coerced", schema: Zoi.object(%{n: Zoi.integer(coerce: true)})})

{:error, live} = Jido.Agent.define(%{name: "live", metadata: %{owner: self()}})

{Exception.message(coerced), Exception.message(live)}
```

The result is
`{"State schemas cannot convert values", "Authoring data cannot contain live values"}`.

Defaults and refinements such as `Zoi.min/2` are allowed. Coercion and
transforms are not, and neither are PIDs, functions, or references in
metadata. This keeps every definition portable data.

## Summary

- One `%Jido.Agent{}` struct has two valid forms: a definition (`id` and
  `state` are `nil`) or an instance (both set and valid).
- The DSL and `Jido.Agent.define/1` produce the same validated definition.
- `instantiate/2` fills defaults and rejects wrong types, unknown keys, and a
  missing `id`.
- Plugins add owned fields. Only the owning Plugin changes them.
- `set/2` updates domain fields. `validate/1` checks a whole value.

